{"authored" [{"question" "q-37362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $N$ given that \n\n$$\nN[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "6bf283bc2cfddc5d9a25b353c7ac44319704195febc4ff79f0c64c7bb355b8ba" "basis" 1427 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $N:$\n\n$$\nN={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $N$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $N$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/state.json" "file_sha256" "6bf283bc2cfddc5d9a25b353c7ac44319704195febc4ff79f0c64c7bb355b8ba" "basis" 1427 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040289/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37362" "field" nil "attribut